# workspace production settings (infra/production/docker-compose.yml). `./install.sh` writes infra/production/.env
# from this file: it asks for the three domain settings and generates every secret. By hand: copy it to .env.
#
# Secrets left empty are generated once by the compose `init` job into the `secrets` volume (backup.sh saves it);
# a value set here always wins. Use long random hex strings (URL-safe; they end up inside connection URLs), e.g.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Postgres passwords take effect on the FIRST start only (empty data volume). Changing them later
# means changing the roles in Postgres too (see README, "Rotating secrets").

# ── Public addresses ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where people open workspace (links in emails, cookies, CSP). https:// in production. Default http://localhost.
APP_URL=https://work.example.com
# Files. Empty: on this server's disk (the `data` volume), served by the server at APP_URL/storage/ with signed,
# expiring links. Set it for S3: the bundled SeaweedFS (add `s3` to COMPOSE_PROFILES, https://<FILES_DOMAIN>) or
# your own S3/R2 (presigned URLs are signed for this host, which browsers and the server must both reach).
# Meeting recordings (LiveKit Egress) need S3. STORAGE_DRIVER=local|s3 overrides the choice.
S3_ENDPOINT=
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local

# Optional profiles, comma-separated: s3 (bundled SeaweedFS), meet (LiveKit), pgbouncer. Without any: postgres, app,
# caddy (the default set).
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# ── Reverse proxy (bundled Caddy, README §5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host name of APP_URL (default localhost, served over plain HTTP). FILES_DOMAIN: host of S3_ENDPOINT with the
# bundled SeaweedFS; empty with local disk storage or your own S3/R2.
# MEET_DOMAIN (LiveKit, --profile meet) and API_DOMAIN (API-key integrations) are optional. DNS must point here.
APP_DOMAIN=work.example.com
FILES_DOMAIN=
# MEET_DOMAIN=meet.example.com
# API_DOMAIN=api.example.com
# TLS=auto: HTTPS with Let's Encrypt certificates Caddy obtains and renews (ports 80 and 443 must reach this server).
# TLS=off: plain HTTP on port 80, when a load balancer or tunnel terminates TLS in front of this server.
# Your own certificate: see infra/production/caddy/Caddyfile.
# TLS=auto
# Let's Encrypt account e-mail (expiry notices); optional.
LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL=
# Published ports of Caddy.
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443

# Interface the internal ports (web 3000, server 4000, S3 8333, LiveKit 7880)
# are published on. Keep 127.0.0.1 with a reverse proxy on this host; 0.0.0.0 exposes them.
BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# ── Secrets (generated when empty, see the top of this file) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Signs the tokens modules call each other with. 32+ characters.
INTERNAL_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Encrypts web sessions. 32+ characters.
SESSION_SECRET=
# Encrypts sign-in secrets at rest: token signing keys, two-step (TOTP) seeds, company IdP client secrets.
# 32+ characters. Changing it signs everyone out and means re-entering the IdP client secret.
AUTH_SECRET=
# Postgres superuser, the owner role (migrations, the event relay's LISTEN, job locks) and the modules' login
# roles (workspace_identity, workspace_work, …: one per schema of the `workspace` database, same password).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
PG_OWNER_PASSWORD=
PG_APP_PASSWORD=
# Object storage keys (S3 only). Bundled SeaweedFS: any values (they become its credentials; generated when empty).
# Own S3 / Cloudflare R2: that provider's keys (and set S3_ENDPOINT, S3_REGION, S3_BUCKET).
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_BUCKET=workspace-files
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ── E-mail (optional: invitations, password reset, the daily digest) ────────────────────────────
# smtp://user:pass@host:587 (STARTTLS) or smtps://user:pass@host:465. URL-encode special characters.
# Without SMTP_URL nothing is emailed: Admin › Members hands over activation links and temporary passwords instead,
# and the daily digest is off. Fill both in whenever a mail relay is available.
SMTP_URL=
MAIL_FROM=workspace <no-reply@example.com>

# ── First-run setup (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /setup (creating the workspace and its owner) asks for this token, so nobody who finds the server before you
# can take it over. Leave empty to have one generated at the first start: `docker compose logs app | grep "setup token"`.
SETUP_TOKEN=

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the modules' pools connect. With `--profile pgbouncer`: PG_HOST=pgbouncer, PG_PORT=6432.
# PG_HOST=postgres
# PG_PORT=5432
# Reverse proxies in front of the web app (per-IP sign-in limits read X-Forwarded-For from the right).
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Public API base for API-key integrations (expose the gateway, e.g. https://api.example.com).
PUBLIC_API_URL=
# Advanced address overrides, all defaulting to APP_URL: where browsers fetch files stored on local disk
# (STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL), the origin the web app's security policy allows for files (STORAGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN, default
# S3_ENDPOINT) and where browsers open the realtime connection (CHAT_PUBLIC_URL). Only for a split setup.
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL=
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=
# CHAT_PUBLIC_URL=
# Days delivered events stay in each module's outbox, the copy `dlq outbox-replay` re-sends from.
# EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=3
# 1 = serve the OpenAPI document and a viewer at /api/docs.
# API_DOCS=1
# Features to leave out of this installation, separated by commas: docs, whiteboards, calendar, time, ai.
# A feature left out disappears from the app (menus, pages, search) and its API answers 404; its data is kept, so
# removing the name and restarting brings it back. Chat and tasks are always on. Empty: everything is on.
#   docs         documents and the file drive          whiteboards  whiteboards
#   calendar     calendar, events, meeting notes       time         attendance and leave
#   ai           the company assistant, AI Insights (browser extension), agents and workflows, the AI section
# DISABLED_FEATURES=whiteboards,calendar
# Abuse limits: sign-ups per IP and hour, the per-IP hourly base of the public auth endpoints (password reset; sign-in,
# token refresh and password set get multiples of it), failed web sign-ins per IP in 15 minutes.
# RATE_SIGNUP_PER_HOUR=10
# RATE_RECOVERY_PER_HOUR=20
# LOGIN_MAX_FAILS_PER_IP=50

# AI (optional; workspace works without it). A vendor is enabled by its key, or OPENAI_BASE_URL for an
# OpenAI-compatible server (Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio, LiteLLM; key optional). With none, every company-AI
# feature is hidden; extension monitoring of ChatGPT/Claude still works. Read by the ai module only.
# Simpler: leave these empty and connect AI in Admin › AI connections (API key, LLM API or self-hosted
# Ollama). An Ollama on this same machine is reachable from the app container as http://host.docker.internal:11434.
# Keys saved there are encrypted with AI_SECRETS_KEY (default: derived from INTERNAL_TOKEN_SECRET).
# AI_SECRETS_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# AI_PROVIDER=gemini
# AI_MODEL_FAST=gemini:gemini-3.8-flash
# AI_MODEL_SMART=gemini:gemini-3.8-flash
# AI_ROUTES={"thread_summary":"gemini:gemini-3.5-flash-lite"}
# AI_FALLBACK=anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5,local:local-extractive

# Video meetings (optional; start with --profile meet). LIVEKIT_URL is what browsers connect to. Leave all three
# unset to run without video meetings and calls (the app hides them; calendar meetings still work).
# Key and secret: any values, the secret 32+ characters (they configure the bundled LiveKit too).
# LIVEKIT_URL=wss://meet.example.com
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=workspace
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=

# Web Push for the morning brief (optional). Generate with `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`.
# The public key is compiled into the web app: run `docker compose build` after changing it.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# MCP servers the company AI may use must be https on the public internet. To allow the company's own internal ones,
# list their hosts (exact names, *.suffix, IP addresses); listed hosts may also use plain http.
# MCP_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS=mcp.corp.internal,10.0.4.12

# Logs are JSON lines on stdout (`docker compose logs app`); who did what is in Admin › Nhật ký.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Image tag built/used by compose.
# WORKSPACE_IMAGE=workspace:local
